1. DBD (Database Description)
Think of DBD as a Table of Contents.
It does not contain the complete routing information.
Typically, a DBD contains:
LSA Headers (summary only)
LSA Type
Link State ID
Advertising Router (Router ID)
LSA Sequence Number
LSA Age
LSA Checksum

2. LSDB (Link-State Database)
Think of the LSDB as the complete Google Maps database.
Typically, the LSDB contains:
Router IDs
Connected Networks
Links between routers
Interface information
Link Costs (Metrics)
Neighbor relationships
LSA Types (Type 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7)
Complete Network Topology


3. Routing Table

Think of the routing table as the final navigation route.
It only contains the best path after SPF (Dijkstra) runs.
Typically, the Routing Table contains:
Destination Network
Subnet Mask / Prefix Length
Next-Hop IP Address
Outgoing Interface
Route Metric (Cost)
Route Source (OSPF, Static, Connected, BGP, etc.)
Example
Destination        Next Hop        Interface    Cost
10.10.10.0/24     192.168.1.2     G0/0         15
172.16.1.0/24     192.168.1.3     G0/1         20